Report a reset connection instead of EINVAL when writing to a socket.
Writes to a socket descriptor go through the C runtime's write(), which
calls WriteFile on the socket handle. When the peer has reset the
connection WriteFile fails with ERROR_NETNAME_DELETED, which the C
runtime does not know and maps to EINVAL, so nc and other applets
printed "write error: Invalid argument". When a write fails with that
error on a pipe-type handle, which includes sockets, set errno to
WSAECONNRESET, as the socket wrappers in net.c store Winsock codes, so
the message becomes "write error: 10054: WSA error".

---
 win32/winansi.c |    6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

--- a/win32/winansi.c
+++ b/win32/winansi.c
@@ -882,6 +882,12 @@
 			exit(128+SIGPIPE);
 		else /* SIG_IGN */
 			errno = EPIPE;
+	} else if (error == ERROR_NETNAME_DELETED &&
+			GetFileType((HANDLE)_get_osfhandle(fd)) == FILE_TYPE_PIPE) {
+		/* A socket whose connection was reset.  The C runtime maps
+		 * this error to EINVAL, so use the Winsock code, as the
+		 * socket functions do. */
+		errno = WSAECONNRESET;
 	}
 }
 
